      "text": "To find the smallest four-digit number that is divisible by \\(33\\), we need to determine the smallest number within the range of four-digit numbers (i.e., from \\(1000\\) to \\(9999\\)) that is divisible by \\(33\\). We can do this by finding the quotient when \\(1000\\) is divided by \\(33\\) and then adjusting to find the smallest four-digit multiple of \\(33\\).\n\nFirst, we perform the division:\n\n\\[\n1000 \\div 33 \\approx 30.3030\n\\]\n\nSince we are looking for the smallest integer multiple of \\(33\\) that is greater than or equal to \\(1000\\), we round up \\(30.3030\\) to the next whole number, which is \\(31\\). Therefore, the smallest integer multiple of \\(33\\) that is greater than or equal to \\(1000\\) is:\n\n\\[\n33 \\times 31 = 1023\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest four-digit number that is divisible by \\(33\\) is \\(\\boxed{1023}\\).\n\nTo verify, we can check if \\(1023\\) is divisible by \\(33\\):\n\n\\[\n1023 \\div 33 = 31\n\\]\n\nSince \\(1023\\) divided by \\(33\\) gives an integer quotient of \\(31\\), it confirms that \\(1023\\) is indeed divisible by \\(33\\). Therefore, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{1023}\\).",
